var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#AASV.jl","page":"Home","text":"Julia implementation of the aasv pipeline for the functional analysis of COI ASVs. The scientific formulas are compatible with R version 0.1.0, commit 9960347948da3142714deb656fcf38124b4e38d8; the architecture uses Julia types, dispatch and conventions.\n\nThis package is part of an experimental effort to use AI to port bioinformatics and quantitative ecology packages to Julia, with the goal of creating a unified environment for high performance scientific computing.\n\nRequires Julia 1.10 or later. Does not require R, Biostrings or DECIPHER.\n\njulia --project=. setup.jl\njulia -t auto --project=. test/runtests.jl\njulia -t auto --project=. examples/offline_pipeline.jl\n\nThe local example creates FASTA files, codon tables, Excel reports and results/demo/analysis/classification.csv.\n\nTo install the version published on GitHub (ProfileAlignments.jl is not registered, so add it first; setup.jl does the same for a local checkout):\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.develop(url=\"https://github.com/alexology/ProfileAlignments.jl\")\nPkg.develop(url=\"https://github.com/alexology/AASV.jl\")\nusing AASV, BioSequences\n\nrecord = Sequence(\"COI\", dna\"ATGCCAGCA\")\nprotein = best_translation(record.sequence)","title":"AASV.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Pipeline-with-real-data","page":"Home","text":"using AASV, DataFrames, CSV\n\ntaxonomy = CSV.read(\"taxonomy.csv\", DataFrame)\n# Required columns: family, genus, species.\nalign_species_seq!(taxonomy; alignment_dir=\"references\",\n                   min_length=500, max_length=1000, genetic_code=\"5\")\n\nfor family in unique(skipmissing(taxonomy.family))\n    problems = check_alignment(family; alignment_dir=\"references\", tax_lev=SpeciesRank)\n    improve_alignment!(problems; log_dir=\"logs\")\n    dir = joinpath(\"references\", \"alignments\", family, \"species\")\n    paths = filter(p -> endswith(p, \".fasta\"), readdir(dir; join=true))\n    filter_divergent_seq!(paths; log_dir=\"logs\", max_distance=0.10)\nend\n\nalign_genus_seq!(; alignment_dir=\"references\")\nalign_family_seq!(; alignment_dir=\"references\")\n# Records whose insertions open gap columns in most of the merged profile\n# are removed and listed in references/logs/gap_causing_references.\n\nasvs = CSV.read(\"asvs.csv\", DataFrame)\n# Required columns: family, genus, species, ASV (DNA sequence).\n# Additional numeric columns: abundances in the samples.\nrevised = asv_functional_structure!(asvs;\n    alignment_dir=\"references\", output_dir=\"analysis\", genetic_code=\"5\")\nverdicts = classify_asv(\"analysis\")\nCSV.write(\"classification.csv\", verdicts)\n\nalign_genus_seq! and align_family_seq! remove, after each merge, the records whose insertions open columns that are gaps in most references (remove_gap_causing=true). R aasv merges without this step and relies on check_alignment and improve_alignment at each level; false reproduces its merges. A few such records otherwise make every query an indel at that level: on 9,768 Scarabaeidae references, 44 records (0.45%) made all 34 tested ASVs INVALID_ORF at family level; with the removal the results matched those obtained with the R references.\n\nThe package precompiles a small offline analysis, so the first call of asv_functional_structure! in a session does not wait for compilation.\n\nJulia options are passed after ;. Missing statistical values use missing; an absent result or option uses nothing. CSV, Excel and DataFrame are input/output adapters, while the core uses concrete structures. Sequences are vectors of Sequence(id, sequence) with unique names. The alignment_dir and output_dir arguments accept Windows, Linux and macOS paths. Use overwrite=true to rebuild references that already exist. Species rebuilds prepare all outputs before installation, remove obsolete species files and invalidate that family's genus/family profiles and curation checkpoints. Rebuild those profiles afterwards with align_genus_seq! and align_family_seq!; other families and non-reference files are preserved.","title":"Pipeline with real data"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Reference-profiles","page":"API","text":"","title":"Reference profiles"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Reference-checks","page":"API","text":"","title":"Reference checks"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Alignment-and-curation","page":"API","text":"","title":"Alignment and curation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#ASV-structure-and-mutations","page":"API","text":"","title":"ASV structure and mutations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Classification","page":"API","text":"","title":"Classification"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Translation-and-similarity","page":"API","text":"","title":"Translation and similarity"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Taxonomic-ranks","page":"API","text":"","title":"Taxonomic ranks"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Shared-with-ProfileAlignments.jl","page":"API","text":"Sequences, alignments and FASTA files are those of ProfileAlignments.jl, re-exported by AASV, so using AASV is enough:\n\nAlignment(records; weights): rectangular alignment of Sequence records, with names, order and normalized positive sequence weights.\nread_fasta(path): read a FASTA file (FASTX.jl) keeping full headers, record order and gap columns; malformed files raise an ArgumentError.\nwrite_fasta(records_or_alignment, path; width = 80): write Sequence records or an Alignment; empty sequences are rejected before the file is created or truncated.","title":"Shared with ProfileAlignments.jl"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#AASV.AASV","page":"API","text":"AASV\n\nFunctional analysis of COI ASVs, a Julia implementation of the R pipeline aasv: reference profiles per species, genus and family (align_species_seq!, align_genus_seq!, align_family_seq!), their curation (improve_alignment!, filter_divergent_seq!), the structural (hydrophobicity) and mutation audit of each ASV (assess_asv, asv_functional_structure!) and the final classification (classify_asv). Sequences and alignments are shared with ProfileAlignments.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.AASV"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.align_family_seq","page":"API","text":"align_family_seq(; kwargs...)\n\nSame as align_family_seq! (name of the R aasv function).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.align_family_seq"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.align_family_seq!","page":"API","text":"Merge genus profiles into family FASTA alignments, largest profile first. show_progress=true reports families, taxa and each profile merge. After merging, records whose insertions open gap columns in most references are removed iteratively (remove_gap_causing=true; R aasv relies on check_alignment and improve_alignment at each level), and listed in log_dir/gap_causing_references/<level>/<family>/<taxon>.tsv (log_dir=nothing disables the report). Otherwise a few such records make every query an indel at that level. Existing profiles are reused unless overwrite=true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.align_family_seq!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.align_genus_seq","page":"API","text":"align_genus_seq(; kwargs...)\n\nSame as align_genus_seq! (name of the R aasv function).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.align_genus_seq"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.align_genus_seq!","page":"API","text":"Merge species profiles into genus FASTA alignments, largest profile first. show_progress=true reports families, taxa and each profile merge. After merging, records whose insertions open gap columns in most references are removed iteratively (remove_gap_causing=true; R aasv relies on check_alignment and improve_alignment at each level), and listed in log_dir/gap_causing_references/<level>/<family>/<taxon>.tsv (log_dir=nothing disables the report). Otherwise a few such records make every query an indel at that level. Existing profiles are reused unless overwrite=true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.align_genus_seq!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.align_species_seq","page":"API","text":"align_species_seq(taxonomy; kwargs...)\n\nSame as align_species_seq! (name of the R aasv function).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.align_species_seq"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.align_species_seq!","page":"API","text":"Download, filter, translate and align species references for all input families. records_provider(family) may supply local BOLD-like records (processid, identification, sequence). Outputs follow the original R directory layout. show_progress=true reports families, downloads, filtering and each species before alignment. Filtering updates use progress_interval seconds (default 5). Complete BOLD pages are cached under alignment_dir/bold_downloads/<family>. With overwrite=false, interrupted downloads resume from that cache, also in a new Julia session; overwrite=true starts a fresh query and download. New species outputs are prepared before installation; obsolete species files, derived genus/family profiles and their curation checkpoints are removed after a successful build. Other families and files outside the managed formats are preserved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.align_species_seq!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.select_shard","page":"API","text":"Select one deterministic, one-based shard from a collection. Useful for scheduler job arrays; every distinct value belongs to exactly one shard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.select_shard"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.check_alignments!","page":"API","text":"Check all reference families and return problematic FASTA paths, including cached results. Checkpoints in alignment_dir/checkpoints/check_alignment are reused only when file names, SHA-256 contents, rank, type and algorithm version match. Set resume=false to repeat checks. Missing/empty rank directories are skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.check_alignments!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.check_reference_records","page":"API","text":"Report reference records that asv_functional_structure! ignores or excludes. Returns a table with family, tax_lev, file, record and issue: \"repeated\" (exact repeat of a record), \"conflicting\" (ID repeated with another sequence; analyses stop on it) or \"no_nucleotide_reference\" (profile record without a nucleotide reference in the taxon, such as another genus in a genus profile or an altered ID written by an earlier aasv version). Genus and family profiles can be rebuilt with overwrite=true. Realigning species files from the raw sequences would restore records removed by earlier curation; rebuild them with align_species_seq!(overwrite=true) instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.check_reference_records"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.filter_divergent_references!","page":"API","text":"Filter all reference families with persistent SHA-256 checkpoints. Returns the current FASTA paths. resume=true reuses completed families only when their output files, max_distance, min_seqs and algorithm version match. Each family's removal report is retained in its checkpoint and written to log_dir/divergent_references/<rank>/<family>.tsv. Filtering operates on the current files; changing thresholds cannot restore previously removed records. Prepared outputs allow interrupted installation to resume without filtering twice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.filter_divergent_references!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.align_profiles","page":"API","text":"Align existing profiles through the standalone Julia ProfileAlignments engine. Defaults: BLOSUM62, affine gapopen=-10/gapextend=-1, free terminal gaps. mode=:query fits the first profile into the second. FFT anchors are optional. Legacy gap selects a linear penalty; match/mismatch select scalar scores.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.align_profiles"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.align_sequences","page":"API","text":"Align sequences using guide-tree progressive alignment and iterative refinement. Uses the independent Julia ProfileAlignments package; preserves IDs/order/duplicates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.align_sequences"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.check_alignment","page":"API","text":"Return paths of FASTA alignments containing internal gaps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.check_alignment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.filter_divergent_seq","page":"API","text":"filter_divergent_seq(paths; kwargs...)\n\nSame as filter_divergent_seq! (name of the R aasv function): the FASTA files in paths are modified in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.filter_divergent_seq"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.filter_divergent_seq!","page":"API","text":"Remove singleton outliers using nearest-neighbour uncorrected amino-acid distance. Terminal gaps are excluded; if every sequence is flagged, the file is retained. Identical sequences share comparisons; duplicates with nongap residues have distance zero. Distances remain exact, including those written to removal logs. show_progress reports file counts and pairwise comparisons every progress_interval seconds (default 5), plus start/completion messages. It defaults to print_messages, but can be enabled independently.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.filter_divergent_seq!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.improve_alignment","page":"API","text":"improve_alignment(paths; kwargs...)\n\nSame as improve_alignment! (name of the R aasv function): the FASTA files in paths are modified in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.improve_alignment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.improve_alignment!","page":"API","text":"Remove gap-causing representatives first, then internally gapped ones. Duplicates are restored. If all candidates are flagged, a sibling species reference breaks the tie; without one, retain the first candidate deterministically.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.improve_alignment!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.re_align","page":"API","text":"re_align(paths; kwargs...)\n\nSame as re_align! (name of the R aasv function): the FASTA files in paths are modified in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.re_align"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.re_align!","page":"API","text":"Realign FASTA alignments in place, without their gaps. With raw_sequences=true, species alignments are rebuilt from raw_sequences, as in R aasv: this resets the curation, restoring records removed by improve_alignment! or filter_divergent_seq!. To rebuild species references, use align_species_seq!(overwrite=true).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.re_align!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AASV.ASVAssessment","page":"API","text":"Typed, in-memory result of an ASV audit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.ASVAssessment"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AASV.ASVInvalidORF","page":"API","text":"ASVInvalidORF\n\nASVStatus: no valid open reading frame, or an alignment with internal gaps; the structural verdict is InvalidORF and no mutations are reported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.ASVInvalidORF"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AASV.ASVStatus","page":"API","text":"ASVStatus\n\nStatus of an ASV audit, stored in ASVAssessment: ASVValid or ASVInvalidORF.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.ASVStatus"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AASV.ASVValid","page":"API","text":"ASVValid\n\nASVStatus: the ASV was translated and aligned to the reference profile without internal gaps; structure and mutations were assessed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.ASVValid"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AASV.HydrophobicityMetrics","page":"API","text":"Typed result of a Kyte-Doolittle calculation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.HydrophobicityMetrics"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AASV.InvalidORF","page":"API","text":"InvalidORF\n\nStructuralVerdict: the query has no valid open reading frame, or its alignment to the reference profile opens internal gaps, so no structural check was made (\"INVALID_ORF\" in the reports).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.InvalidORF"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AASV.MutationObservation","page":"API","text":"One amino-acid difference with nucleotide and reference coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.MutationObservation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AASV.StructuralVerdict","page":"API","text":"StructuralVerdict\n\nVerdict of a hydrophobicity check (assess_structure), stored in StructureAssessment. Values: StructurePass, StructureFail, StructureUnavailable, InvalidORF.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.StructuralVerdict"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AASV.StructureAssessment","page":"API","text":"Typed structural assessment used by the computational core.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.StructureAssessment"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AASV.StructureFail","page":"API","text":"StructureFail\n\nStructuralVerdict: the global score lies outside the reference bounds or too many positions fall outside the local envelope (\"FAIL\" in the reports).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.StructureFail"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AASV.StructurePass","page":"API","text":"StructurePass\n\nStructuralVerdict: the global Kyte-Doolittle score lies within the reference bounds and the fraction of positions outside the local envelope is below violation_threshold (\"PASS\" in the reports).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.StructurePass"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AASV.StructureUnavailable","page":"API","text":"StructureUnavailable\n\nStructuralVerdict: the check could not be made because the global score or every position of the local profile is missing (\"UNAVAILABLE\" in the reports).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.StructureUnavailable"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.assess_asv","page":"API","text":"Audit a query against an amino-acid reference profile; report novel residues, codon coordinates, Grantham distances, ORF incompatibilities and hydrophobicity. Optional VSEARCH mapping uses Phred+33 error probabilities for read-back quality, from the FASTQ files of asv_samples. sample_names lists every sample of the study, so that a file matching several names is rejected (default asv_samples).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.assess_asv"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.assess_structure","page":"API","text":"Compare query hydrophobicity against reference bounds without table allocation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.assess_structure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.asv_functional_structure","page":"API","text":"asv_functional_structure(asv_taxonomy; output_dir, alignment_dir, kwargs...)\n\nSame as asv_functional_structure! (name of the R aasv function).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.asv_functional_structure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.asv_functional_structure!","page":"API","text":"Analyze every available species/genus/family reference for an ASV taxonomy table. Required columns: family, genus, species, ASV. Returns sorted taxonomy with ASV IDs. Writes Excel reports and query FASTA alignments; numeric columns are sample abundances.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.asv_functional_structure!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.calculate_asv_distance","page":"API","text":"Tabular compatibility adapter for assess_asv, used by report writers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.calculate_asv_distance"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.check_asv_structure","page":"API","text":"Tabular compatibility adapter for assess_structure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.check_asv_structure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.get_hydro_metrics","page":"API","text":"Kyte–Doolittle mean and sliding-window profile. Unknown residues yield missing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.get_hydro_metrics"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AASV.ClassificationConfig","page":"API","text":"Configuration for the Functionality Index calculation. Pass this value positionally to classify_asv when the same policy is reused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.ClassificationConfig"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.classify_asv","page":"API","text":"Compute the R package's COI Functionality Index from Excel or TSV reports. FI = 1 - (0.35 × mean site penalty + 0.30 × codon penalty + 0.35 × hydro). Hard ORF incompatibilities (aa_pos=-1) force FI=0. Low-quality mutations are excluded; missing quality is accepted. This score measures compatibility with functional COI evolution and does not establish mitochondrial/NUMT origin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.classify_asv"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AASV.Translation","page":"API","text":"A stop-free forward translation and its one-based reading frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.Translation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.aa_similarity_matrix","page":"API","text":"Tabular compatibility view of codon_similarity_matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.aa_similarity_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.aa_table","page":"API","text":"aa_table(records; genetic_code=\"5\") -> DataFrame\n\nCodon-by-codon translation of each record, in the reading frame chosen by find_translation (records without a valid frame are skipped). Columns: seq_id, position (codon number), triplet, aa. The default genetic code 5 is the invertebrate mitochondrial code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.aa_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.best_translation","page":"API","text":"Compatibility adapter returning the sequence or frame, with missing on failure. New code should use find_translation, whose return type does not depend on an option.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.best_translation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.codon_similarity_matrix","page":"API","text":"Dense 64x64 codon Hamming-similarity matrix in CODONS order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.codon_similarity_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AASV.find_translation","page":"API","text":"Return the first stop-free forward translation, or nothing when none exists.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.find_translation"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AASV.FamilyRank","page":"API","text":"FamilyRank\n\nTaxonomicRank of family profiles merged from genus profiles (:family or \"family\" in keyword arguments).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.FamilyRank"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AASV.GenusRank","page":"API","text":"GenusRank\n\nTaxonomicRank of genus profiles merged from species references (:genus or \"genus\" in keyword arguments).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.GenusRank"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AASV.SpeciesRank","page":"API","text":"SpeciesRank\n\nTaxonomicRank of species references (:species or \"species\" in keyword arguments).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.SpeciesRank"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AASV.TaxonomicRank","page":"API","text":"Taxonomic rank used by reference and ASV workflows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.TaxonomicRank"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AASV.Sequence","page":"API","text":"Sequence(id, sequence)\n\nNamed sequence, normalized to uppercase ASCII; alignment gaps use -. The sequence and FASTA API (Sequence, Alignment, read_fasta, write_fasta) is shared with ProfileAlignments.jl (ProfileAlignments.Sequence).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.Sequence"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AASV.ScoringModel","page":"API","text":"ScoringModel\n\nScoring model of the profile aligner, shared with ProfileAlignments.jl (ProfileAlignments.ScoringModel): substitution matrix, gap penalties and alignment mode. Pass it as model to align_sequences and align_profiles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AASV.ScoringModel"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Local-checks-and-provenance","page":"Local checks and provenance","text":"","title":"Local checks and provenance"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Local-checks","page":"Local checks and provenance","text":"The tests cover FASTA, translation, gaps, alignment cleaning, distances, Phred quality, FI, simulated BOLD retries and the complete offline pipeline. The optional comparison with R checks every numeric column and the class across eight scenarios, as well as translation, hydrophobicity and genetic codes:\n\njulia --project=. test/parity.jl 'C:\\Program Files\\R\\R-4.5.0\\bin\\Rscript.exe'\n\nThis check requires R with dplyr, readxl, Biostrings, Peptides and the original sources under reference/aasv (or an alternative path as the second argument). Normal use and the Julia tests do not require R. The included CI runs the tests on Windows and Linux, with Julia 1.10 and the stable release. The alignment package also has its own suite, which can be run on its own:\n\njulia --project=../ProfileAlignments.jl -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'","title":"Local checks"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Provenance","page":"Local checks and provenance","text":"This port derives from aasv, by Alex Laini, Samuele Voyron and Laura Gruppuso, distributed under GPL >=2. This port is also distributed under GPL >=2. The Grantham table comes from the grantham package, MIT licence; see THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md. The copies of the original repositories in reference/ are local comparison material and are excluded from Git.","title":"Provenance"},{"category":"section","location":"vsearch/#VSEARCH-read-quality","page":"VSEARCH read quality","text":"","title":"VSEARCH read quality"},{"category":"section","location":"vsearch/#Read-quality-with-VSEARCH","page":"VSEARCH read quality","text":"asv_functional_structure(asvs;\n    alignment_dir=\"references\", output_dir=\"analysis\",\n    vsearch_path=raw\"C:\\tools\\vsearch.exe\",\n    trimmed_folder=\"trimmed\", ee=40)\n\nSamples are selected from the numeric columns with abundance >0. A FASTQ file belongs to a sample when the sample name is a whole token of the file name, bounded by its ends or by non-alphanumeric characters: S1 selects S1.fastq and trimmed_S1_R1.fq, not S10.fastq (R aasv matches any substring). A file matching several sample names stops the analysis before any ASV is processed; samples without files are reported. Uncompressed FASTQ files (.fastq, .fq) with Phred+33 qualities are supported. Error probabilities are 10^(-Q/10); the ee filter uses their sum, and codon quality is 1-sum(errors) clamped to [0,1]. VSEARCH errors stop the analysis. The probability computation is tested locally; integration with a real VSEARCH executable requires installing it.","title":"Read quality with VSEARCH"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Using-AASV-from-Julia","page":"Using AASV from Julia","text":"","title":"Using AASV from Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Julia-API","page":"Using AASV from Julia","text":"Sequence is the same type exported by ProfileAlignments: alignments no longer copy each record between two domain representations. The typed translation returns a single result type:\n\ntranslation = find_translation(dna\"ATGCCAGCA\")\nif !isnothing(translation)\n    translation.amino_acids\n    translation.frame\nend\n\nassess_structure and assess_asv return StructureAssessment and ASVAssessment respectively; amino acid differences are MutationObservation values. These APIs compose the computation in memory without creating intermediate tables. check_asv_structure and calculate_asv_distance keep the tabular output for the existing reports.\n\nTaxonomic options accept SpeciesRank, GenusRank and FamilyRank. A reusable FI policy is represented by an immutable value:\n\npolicy = ClassificationConfig(quality_threshold=0.995,\n                              include_hydro=false)\nverdicts = classify_asv(\"analysis\", policy)\n\nFunctions that modify files or directories end with !: align_species_seq!, align_genus_seq!, align_family_seq!, re_align!, improve_alignment!, filter_divergent_seq! and asv_functional_structure!. The previous names remain as adapters for compatibility with existing code.\n\nfilter_divergent_seq! reports progress over files and, every 5 seconds, over the sequence comparisons in the current file. To show it even with print_messages=false, pass show_progress=true; the frequency is set with progress_interval (in seconds). The distance computation works directly on the bytes of the ASCII sequences, without copying rows for each pair, and precomputes the gap-free ends. Identical sequences share their comparisons while keeping all the original identifiers. Distances and removal rules remain exact, including cases with no overlap or with gaps only. The gain grows with the number of duplicates; when all sequences differ, the maximum number of comparisons remains quadratic.\n\nTo process all families without writing loops in the script:\n\nspecies_bad = check_alignments!(; alignment_dir=reference_dir,\n    tax_lev=SpeciesRank)\nisempty(species_bad) || improve_alignment!(species_bad)\n\n# After the fix, only the modified families are checked again.\nspecies_still_bad = check_alignments!(; alignment_dir=reference_dir,\n    tax_lev=SpeciesRank)\n\nfilter_divergent_references!(; alignment_dir=reference_dir,\n    tax_lev=SpeciesRank, max_distance=0.10)\n\nBoth functions discover the families and skip directories without FASTA files. families=[\"FamilyA\", \"FamilyB\"] restricts the selection. resume=true (default) reuses the results saved in alignment_dir/checkpoints only if the file names and SHA-256 contents, the parameters and the algorithm version all match. New downloads, added or removed files and realignments also invalidate the results when the content changes. resume=false forces a new run.\n\nOn a cluster, align_species_seq!, align_genus_seq!, align_family_seq!, check_alignments! and filter_divergent_references! accept shard_index and shard_count. Families are sorted and partitioned deterministically, without overlaps. For example, an 8-element job array uses shard_index=task_id, shard_count=8. Jobs may share alignment_dir, because each shard writes distinct families and checkpoints; downloads or unsharded runs must not be started in parallel on the same files.\n\nasv_functional_structure! instead uses dynamic tasks within the node and accepts threads=:auto or an integer limit. ASV computations run in parallel in batches bounded by the number of threads, while the aggregated reports are assembled in the same order as the input. Each reference profile and its nucleotide references are read once per run and released after the last ASV of the family; reference codons are translated only at novel residues.\n\nasv_functional_structure! also reads reference sets built by earlier aasv versions. Exact repeats of a record (same ID and sequence) are ignored; an ID repeated with a different sequence is an error. Profile records without a nucleotide reference in the taxon cannot be checked codon by codon and are excluded, together with the columns left with gaps only. This removes, for example, the Canthonidia species that an earlier R version merged into the Canthon genus profile. Both cases are reported once per file with a warning. The other functions that read references (check_alignment, improve_alignment!, re_align!, filter_divergent_seq! and the genus/family merges) also ignore exact repeats, and a family merge keeps a species found in two genus profiles once. check_reference_records(; alignment_dir) lists these records in a table before an analysis:\n\nissues = check_reference_records(; alignment_dir=\"references\")\n\nGenus and family profiles can be rebuilt with overwrite=true. Species alignments with altered IDs need align_species_seq!(overwrite=true): realigning them from raw_sequences would restore records removed by earlier curation. check_alignments! also returns the problematic paths of reused families; it additionally supports GenusRank, FamilyRank and type=\"raw_sequences\".\n\nThe filter records the file fingerprints after modification and stores the summary of removed sequences in the checkpoints. Per-family reports are written to alignment_dir/logs/divergent_references/<level>/<family>.tsv (customisable with log_dir, or disabled with log_dir=nothing). Resumed runs do not duplicate report rows. Outputs are prepared before the FASTA files are replaced: an interrupted replacement can be completed on the next run without repeating the filter. If the interruption happens during the computation, the family in progress is recomputed; completed families are reused. Staging files remain available in case of interruption. The filter works on the current files: a less restrictive threshold does not restore previously removed sequences; to do that, regenerate the references. show_progress=false disables the progress messages.\n\nBy default, align_species_seq! reports the families processed or reused, progress through BOLD pages, filtering, and the species being aligned with its number of sequences. Pass show_progress=false to disable these messages; progress_interval controls updates during filtering. Alignment messages are emitted before each species. Independent species of the same family are aligned in batches with tasks from the separate ProfileAlignments package; each batch is limited to the number of available threads to bound memory use and avoid nested parallelism. Alignment algorithms, presets and numerical structures live in the ProfileAlignments package; AASV handles downloads, taxonomy and files.\n\nalign_genus_seq! and align_family_seq! report the families and taxa processed or reused, the number of sequences in the profiles to merge, the completed merges with the percentage for the current taxon, and the final time. Updates are emitted before and after each merge; show_progress=false disables them.","title":"Julia API"},{"category":"section","location":"differences/#Ported-functions-and-differences","page":"Ported functions and differences","text":"","title":"Ported functions and differences"},{"category":"section","location":"differences/#Ported-functions","page":"Ported functions and differences","text":"Function Operation\naa_similarity_matrix 4096 codon pairs with Hamming similarity\nalign_species_seq! BOLD COI-5P download, filters, translation, alignments\nalign_genus_seq!, align_family_seq! progressive profile merging\ncheck_alignment paths of alignments with internal gaps\ncheck_alignments! check of all families with persistent results\nfilter_divergent_references! per-family filter with resume and persistent reports\nre_align! realignment, optionally from the original FASTA files\nimprove_alignment! iterative removal of problematic sequences, with logs\nfilter_divergent_seq! nearest-neighbour distance filter\nasv_functional_structure! ASV analysis at the three taxonomic levels\nclassify_asv FI index, class, evidence and intermediate metrics\n\nAlso available: read_fasta, write_fasta, best_translation, aa_table, align_sequences, align_profiles, calculate_asv_distance, get_hydro_metrics and check_asv_structure. ?function_name shows the documentation in Julia.","title":"Ported functions"},{"category":"section","location":"differences/#Compatibility-and-differences","page":"Ported functions and differences","text":"The FI formula, the weights (0.35/0.30/0.35), the 0.90 threshold, the quality filter, the normalised entropy and the conservation discount 1-exp(-n_ref/20) follow the R code. Grantham distances use the original table, not an approximation. Hydrophobicity uses the Kyte–Doolittle scale.\nAlignment uses the independent ProfileAlignments.jl package, installed as a sibling project and written entirely in Julia: BLOSUM62, affine gaps (gap_open=-10, gap_extend=-1), k-mer distances, guide tree, two progressive passes and iterative refinement. Terminal gaps are free; ASV queries are inserted into the profile with mode=:query. anchors=:fft enables the approximate FFT acceleration, while the default uses full dynamic programming. The previous linear-penalty engine has been replaced. It does not reproduce DECIPHER or MAFFT: coordinates, gaps, mutations and FI may differ. Biological validation on real benchmarks is still required.\nSequence, best_translation and the protein metrics accept BioSequences types directly (LongDNA, LongAA and the dna\"...\", aa\"...\" literals). The pipeline converts these inputs once into the internal ASCII encoding, keeping compatibility with the files and with the string API.\nDECIPHER-specific arguments (for example processors, terminalGap) do not carry over directly; use the Julia parameters listed above. The previous arguments match, mismatch and gap are still accepted: gap sets a linear penalty. The filter distance is the fraction of differing residues in the intersection of the covered regions, excluding terminal gaps.\nBuilt-in genetic codes: the 20 codes of the Biostrings table used in the R comparison (1–6, 9–16, 21–26), without reinterpreting start codons. For other codes, pass a complete codon→amino acid dictionary. IUPAC bases that translate unambiguously produce the corresponding residue; otherwise X. Translation searches only the three forward frames, ignores the incomplete terminal codon and does not search the reverse complement.\nIdentical profiles are deduplicated for the structural analysis, as in R. When restoring an entirely problematic alignment without an external reference, the first sequence is kept deterministically, whereas R picks one at random.\nBOLD is read through the paginated API, even for small families. Retries keep the backoff state; permanent HTTP 4xx errors stop the download immediately. A .complete marker prevents a directory created during a failed download from being treated as complete. Downloaded pages are saved to alignment_dir/bold_downloads/<family> only once fully received. With overwrite=false, a rerun reuses the local pages and requests only the missing pages of the same query; overwrite=true starts a new query without mixing pages from different downloads. The cache is kept after completion. If BOLD no longer serves the saved query, restart with overwrite=true. For local data, pass records_provider=family -> records to align_species_seq.\nThe analysis also records comparisons without mutations or whose hydrophobicity cannot be computed, so no ASV is lost from the reports. When the hydrophobicity metric is missing, its FI contribution is zero, as in R; the report contains UNAVAILABLE. A stop codon or an incompatible gap produces the sentinel aa_pos=-1 and an FI of zero.\nReports keep the main names and columns of the R version. classify_asv reads Excel or TSV. Re-running the pipeline replaces the previous reports in the aa_structure_results directory.\n\nThe FI index measures compatibility with the evolution of functional COI; it is neither a probability nor a diagnosis of NUMT/mitochondrial origin.","title":"Compatibility and differences"}]
}
